// PARAM: --analysis interval --solver warrow
// n is concretely 1 or 2, so n < 3 genuinely holds -- but Apinis warrowing
// cannot establish it. Mechanism: p's single FunctionEntry seed is written by
// two distinct call-site origins. The first write leaves the slot at [1,1];
// the second recomputes the join as [1,2] and widens [1,1] against it, and
// since the upper bound grew that bound goes to +inf. Neither producer's own
// contribution ever moved -- the growth is an artifact of widening a value
// already joined across origins.
//
// Not a limitation of widening as such: 15-solver-choice/precision/
// 06-warrow_per_origin_keeps_bound.vimp is the same program under
// --solver warrow-per-origin, which widens per origin and answers PROVED.
//
// The same four checks as the exact cases, read against a widened slot. Only
// `0 < n` survives: `n < 3` is UNKNOWN precisely because the upper bound is
// gone, which is the difference this case exists to record. Pinning it this
// way keeps the contrast with the join and per-origin cases legible.
void p(int32 n) {
  __voblint_check(0 < n);   // PROVED
  __voblint_check(n < 3);   // UNKNOWN
  __voblint_check(1 < n);   // UNKNOWN
  __voblint_check(n < 2)    // UNKNOWN
}
void main() {
  p(1);
  p(2)
}
